# McFarland - 2016 - Diagnostic Approach to Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes

- **link:** <https://docs.google.com/document/d/1v95PCSUrC5TnxBsa_qnb6AgXjaf0a2DlHNd8CgE_QME/edit?tab=t.0>
- **Most atypical parkinsonian syndromes are in fact "neuroproteinopathies"** - deposition of abnormal protein in neural structures:
  - <u>Synucleinopathies</u> - multiple system atrophy
  - <u>Tauopathies</u> - progressive supranuclear palsy, corticobasal degeneration
  - <u>Amyloidopathies</u> - corticobasal degeneration
- **Red flag features suggestive of atypical parkisonian syndrome:** 
![](../img/red_flag_features_for_atypical_parkinsonism.png)
  - <u>Rapid disease trajectory</u> - all forms of APS exhibits aggressive neurodegeneration typically leading to severe disability or death in a window of \< 10y
  - <u>Symmetrical progression fof disease</u> - suggestive of PSP, and MSA
  - <u>Absence of pharmacolognical responses</u> - IPD a/w robust response to LD, but primarily failure to adequate trial of LD, a rapidly-lost response, or medication-induced dysathesia suggestive of an atypical syndrome
  - <u>Early gait instability and unexplained falls</u> - onset of severe unexplained falls within 1-2y of Dx is highly specific for APS, and is most common in PSP
  - <u>Absence of tremors</u> - absence of tremors is highly atypical of IPD, where APS may frequently present w/o any tremors, or tremors atypical of IPD (e.g. myoclonus)
  - <u>Other neurological deficits</u>:
    - Pyramidal signs - suggestive of MSA
    - Cerebellar signs - suggestive of MSA
    - Bulbar dysfunction (severe dysphonia/ dysarthria) - suggestive of MSA or PSP
    - Cortical dysfunction (e.g. apraxia/ myoclonus) - suggestive of CBD
    - Early prominent dysautonomia - suggestive of MSA
    - Supranuclear gaze palsies (absence voluntary movement w/ movement of doll-eye reflexes) - suggestive of PSP but also seen in CBD
    - Gait - wide-based gait rather than narrow shuffling gait suggestive of PSP
    - Early cognitive impairment - suggestive of LBD, CBD, PSP
- **Progressive supranuclear palsy:**
  - **Pathophysiology** - tauopathy:
    - Classically affects the midbrain, superior cerebellar peduncles and sparing the cortex resulting in Richardson syndrome
    - Various cortical involvement may be observed
  - **Natural Hx of PSP:**
    - Onset strictly after 40y, w/ mean age of onset at 63-66y
    - Mean survival of 5-8y
    - Mortality primarily driven by bulbar complications or from frequent falls
  - **Clinical features of PSP:**
    - **Early postural instability and unexplained falls** (\< 1-2y of Dx) - paramount features distinguishing PSP from other syndromes driven by profound axial rigidity, bradykinesia, freezing of gait, loss of postural reflexes, visual-vestibular disconnects, and a fatal loss of insight (the rocket sign)
    - **Features of occular dysfunction:**
      - <u>Reading difficulty</u> - due to inability of scanning texts, and partially affected by blurred vision
      - <u>Diplopia</u> - almost invariable but also seen in other atypical parkisonism due to convergence insufficiency
      - <u>Photosensitivity and corneal irritation</u> - due to loss of blinking resulting in corneal/ conjunctival irritaitons
    - **Bulbar and facial features:**
      - <u>Worried appearance</u> - may be described by family members (see below)
      - <u>Progressive dysarthria</u> - mixed spastic, hypernasal, hypokinetic, and monotonous speech pattern; and is often slow, fraught w/ stuttering, echolalia, involuntary vocalization and apraxia of phonation
      - <u>Progressive dysphagia</u> - leading to increased risk of aspiration and early mortality
    - **Neuropsychiatric features** - cognitive decline as frontal-subcortical dementia w/ emotional lability or pseudobulbar affect (most common in PSP)
  - **Signs of PSP:**
    - **General appearance:**
      - <u>Astonished, worried facies</u> - arises from the hypertonic facial musculature resulting in facial folds and rigid bradykinesia
      - <u>Procerus signs</u> - eyebrow furrowing and vertical wrinkles over glabella due to sustained procerus contraction
    - **Progressive supranuclear opthalmoparesis** - results in inability for volitional saccades and pursuits, which can be overcome by vestibular or cervical-ocular reflexes:
      - <u>Reduced vertical gaze</u> - limitation of doward gaze is more Sn/Sp; limitation of upward gaze is usually non-specific
      - <u>Reduced horizontal gaze</u> (and complete opthalmoplegia) - as the syndrome progresses to involve both vertical and horizontal palsies
      - <u>Slowed scaccades and reduced optokinetic nystagmus</u> - slowed saccades resulting in pursuits to appear less smooth, usualy verticle more affected than horizontal
      - <u>Square-wave jerks</u> - rapid back and forth mini saccadic intrusions during fixation
  - **Progressive supranuclear palsy syndromes:** 
  ![](../img/progressive_supranuclear_palsy_syndromes.png)
  - **Dx** - neuroimaging remains most reliable supportive ancilliary tests:
    - <u>MRI brain</u> - profound atrophy of midprain w/ relative preservation of pons (hummingbird sign)
    - <u>Functional imaging</u> - emerging use of PET/SPECT to reveal hypometabolism and detect tau deposition
- **Corticobasal degeneration and corticobasal syndrome:**
  - **Clinical features of PSP** - parkinson-like features remain clinically asymmetrical although wide-spread cortical involvement on MRI:
    - **Motor dysfunction:**
      - <u>Parkinsonian-like features</u> - insiduous onset of asymmetrical hand clumsiness, early focal bradykinesia, rigidity, but may be characterised by a coarse resting/ action tremor instead of the characteristic find tremor
      - <u>Dystonia</u> - involuntary muscle contractions late in disease course resulting in painful, fixed contractures which is disabling
      - <u>Alien-limb phenomenon</u> - limb appears to be having a mind of its own, in pursuit of tactile stimulus, affecting activity of the normal limb, and once reported to cause strangulation of individuals neck
    - **Graphesthesia** - reflects cortical sensory loss, w/ intact peripheral nerve pathways
    - **Ideomotor apraxia** - absolute inability to perform a skilled, or learned motor task, despite completely intact motory strength, cognitive ability and language comprehension
    - **Cognitive impairment** - late feature unlike in PSP
  - **Signs of CBD:**
    - Graphasthesia
    - Dystonia
    - Coarse tremors
  - **Phenotypes of corticobasal syndrome:** 
  ![](../img/corticobasal_syndrome_phenotypes.png)
  - **Dx** - clinical Dx of CBS, rarely correlating to CBD pathology of symmetrical cerebral atrophy
